---
description: Authoring rules for Bloomy precalculus lessons
alwaysApply: true
---

# Bloomy lesson authoring

- Accuracy first. Base every definition, formula, and worked example on reliable sources such as standard precalculus textbooks or credible references. Do not invent notation or conventions.
- No em dashes anywhere: prose, narration, UI copy, or comments. Use commas, colons, parentheses, or separate sentences.
- No semicolons in any learner-facing copy: narration, practice lines, question prompts, choices, explanations, hints, figure labels, and website UI text. Prefer a period and two short sentences when it joins two independent clauses, a comma for a list or aside, or a colon when the second part explains the first. Never write a comma splice. This is enforced by `npm run check` (the only allowed semicolon in copy is the KaTeX spacing command `\;` inside `$...$`). Code and CSS syntax semicolons are unaffected.
- Voice: academic, precise, and genuinely engaged with the mathematics. Write flowing, complete sentences in an expository register, like a strong lecturer or a well written textbook, not a chatty assistant. Enthusiasm comes from the ideas and the momentum of the explanation, never from filler, exclamation, or cute asides. See PRODUCT.md.
- Be explicit and literal. State exactly what changes and exactly why, naming the mechanism every time. Banned as vague causal filler: "the geometry does the work", "makes this concrete", "here is the catch", "here is the key picture", "the trick is", "confirms the idea", "as we can see", "it turns out", "notice how", "think of it as". Replace each with the specific fact, for example "squaring -4 gives +16, so the negative sign disappears".
- Make it flow, do not stutter. No sentence fragments used for punch (for example "Same length, new split." or "The whole length went vertical."). No stacks of comma separated appositives, meaning three or more commas piling qualifiers onto one noun. Join ideas with real subordination (because, since, when, so that, which, whose) so each sentence reads as one connected thought, and keep only the commas grammar requires.
- No cutesy or mechanical metaphors for the math ("dials", "drive", "bring it home", "drop it onto the grid", "swing it"). Name the actual action, such as "rotate the vector" or "place the point at".
- Keep beats short. Beats accumulate on the slide as they play, so thick paragraphs pile into a wall. Aim for two or three lean sentences per beat (one is fine), cut restatement and UI narration ("the readout shows..."), and keep only the essential explicit content. Tighten wording rather than adding more beats.
- Never make the learner scroll the left narration column. The beats on a slide stack up as they play, so if the accumulated text would overflow the panel, split the material across a new slide at a natural conceptual break rather than cramming one long slide. As a rough budget, keep a slide to at most five or six short beats and roughly 1200 characters of narration total. When you split, carry every reveal flag that was on at the end of the first slide into the second slide's `baseReveal`, keep the same `mode` and params, give the new slide a unique `id`, and move or share the try questions so each slide still has something to do. Verify with `node scripts/voice-scan.mjs --lesson <id>`.
- Not every narration beat needs motion. If a beat has nothing to animate, leave `to` and `draw` off, let it read as text, and move on to the next beat.
- Introduce one visual change at a time. Ask the learner to predict or manipulate before revealing an answer. Always offer a hint and a graceful way forward.
- Do not instruct interaction during the watch stage. Save "drag", "slide", "click", or "try" prompts for the try stage (the `practice` line and questions), which appear only once "Your turn" begins. Watch beats narrate and may describe automated motion, but never tell the learner to touch the figure.
- Concept checks stay on the question. A choice-only Your turn is the question box. Leave `practice` empty and do not leave a recap above the prompt. The player hides leftover practice and sliders on choice items, highlights the left panel, and dims the figure the same way watch-stage text does. Keep `practice`, sliders, and a full-opacity figure only when the current item is a manipulate or plot.
- Never break a working lesson. Reuse the shared player and engine and the existing template. Keep the unit circle behavior intact.
- Narration fades in a whole paragraph at a time (not word by word). Keep attention on the text while it reads, and only shift focus to the figure after the beat finishes.
- Render math with KaTeX. Write math in beats and copy using `$...$` (see the bloomy-figures rule).
- Prefer worked examples with concrete numbers and a visible coordinate plane when it makes the idea clearer.
- Over-explain by default. Assume this is the learner's first exposure and actively anticipate exactly where a beginner gets stuck, then address it right there, in the same beat. Define every new term the instant it appears (conjugate, critical point, wall, multiplicity, interval notation, etc.), justify why each step is allowed, and never skip an algebra step. Walk through worked examples one concrete case at a time (for a sign chart, narrate each region and each factor's sign). When unsure whether to add a clarifying sentence, add it. A confused or frustrated learner is a failure, not an edge case: err on the side of too much explanation, never too little.
- Pencil-mimic principle (the main idea). Everything on screen should be something the learner could reproduce with a pencil: number lines, sign charts, labeled points, mirrored roots. The figure is a live version of the hand procedure, not a black box. Avoid effects that cannot be drawn by hand.
- Be explicit about procedure. Name the steps out loud ("the method has three steps: find the critical points, test each interval, keep the sign you want") and use direct "make sure to ___" callouts at the moment they matter (for example, "make sure each test point sits strictly inside an interval"). Use the learner's vocabulary: critical points, zeros, asymptotes/walls.
- Show the work, factor by factor. For any sign analysis, display the sign of each factor per region as a chart (a row per interval, a column per factor, plus the product), and put the current region in focus as the tracer moves. Also annotate the expression itself with the sign above each parenthesized factor.
- Keep the equations dynamic, even when there is no graph. An algebra or identity derivation writes itself line by line: each step is introduced by a labeled arrow naming the move (expand, substitute, cancel, divide, factor), cancelling terms are struck (`\cancel`, with the reduced quotient shown by `\overset`/`\underset`), key terms and signs are colour-highlighted, each line fades and slides in, and the result lands in a highlighted box. Keep the current (newest) equation center-ish in the step panel so it never drops off the bottom. Older lines scroll up and fade out at the top. Do not scale the whole derivation to fit on one screen. Leave a visible shaft above each op chip. Do not skip the intermediate form (show $360^\circ/2 = 2\pi/2$ before $180^\circ = \pi$). Each step must transform the line above it, or the arrow label must name the line it actually uses. Use the shared `AlgebraFlow` component with a small supporting figure glyph; never leave a wall of static text. The reference look is `degrees-radians`.
- Climb and Summit stay inside the lesson. Every quiz item must be answerable from that lesson's watch and try stages, plus ordinary Algebra 2 arithmetic and facts the lesson itself uses as given. If a formula or procedure is not derived or stated in the watch, do not quiz it. Rewrite or drop the item rather than introducing it first on Climb or Summit.
- Questions must never start already answered. For a manipulate/tracer question, set the starting value OUTSIDE the correct region so the learner has to move to solve it; never let the resting position satisfy the check.
- Every interactive control must visibly move something. A slider/tracer must drive a visible point (and readout) on the figure; if dragging changes nothing on screen, it is a bug.
- Accessibility: support keyboard, pointer, and touch, keep visible focus and readable contrast, and provide reduced-motion fallbacks. Target WCAG 2.2 AA.
